category: science

entry radioactive-contamination
term: Radioactive Contamination
related_terms: irradiation
related_terms: exposure
related_terms: radiation
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
quick_facts: Contamination is radioactive material on or in something
quick_facts: Radiation itself does not spread; the material does
quick_facts: Can be on skin or clothes, breathed in or swallowed, or spread about a place
quick_facts: A contaminated person is exposed until the material is removed
quick_facts: Material on skin or clothes can often be washed off

sources:
    {"organization":"Health Physics Society","title":"What Is Radioactive Contamination?","url":"https://hps.org/ate_faq/radcontamination/"}
    {"organization":"World Health Organization","title":"Ionizing radiation and health effects","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/ionizing-radiation-and-health-effects"}

definition:
    Radioactive material that has been deposited on materials, skin, clothing or any place where it is not wanted, or that has been breathed in, swallowed or absorbed through a wound. A person is externally contaminated if the material is on the skin or clothing, and internally contaminated if it is inside the body. The environment is contaminated if radioactive material is spread about or is unconfined. If radioactive material is not in a sealed source container, it might be spread onto other objects.

debunking_snippet:
    “Radiation spreads onto people.” Radiation does not get on or in people. It is radioactive contamination, the material itself, that can be spread.
